    <h3>2. Akari (Light Up)</h3>

    <p>Akari, known outside Japan as Light Up, is the grid puzzle for people who like their deduction with a bit of atmosphere. You are given a grid of white and black cells, and you place light bulbs on white cells so that every white cell ends up illuminated, while no two bulbs ever shine on each other in the same unobstructed row or column. Black cells block light, and numbered black cells specify exactly how many bulbs must touch them. Every placement ripples outward: one bulb lights its row and column and simultaneously forbids a whole set of future placements, which gives Akari the feel of a gentle chess game against yourself. The endgame has a lovely payoff too, when the last few dark cells can only be lit one way and the whole board resolves in a chain of forced moves. Akari rewards patience and careful scanning more than raw speed, and it pairs beautifully with Shikaku since the two puzzles use similar grids for completely different goals, one dividing space and the other illuminating it.</p>

    <div class="cta-box">
      <p>🐱 Or try lighting up the grid: <a href="/akari/">Play Akari free →</a></p>
    </div>

    <h3>3. Sudoku</h3>

    <p>Sudoku needs no introduction, but it deserves an accurate one. Fill a 9 by 9 grid so that every row, every column, and every 3 by 3 box contains the digits 1 through 9 exactly once. That is the entire rule set, and it generates a staggering amount of logical depth: easy puzzles resolve through simple scanning, while hard ones demand techniques with names like naked pairs, X-wings, and swordfish. Sudoku was popularized by Nikoli in Japan in the 1980s and exploded worldwide in 2004 when The Times of London began printing it, a run on puzzle books followed, and it has never really left. Its great strength is availability: it is everywhere, free, in every difficulty, and its constraint structure is so clean that mathematicians have studied it seriously, with the minimum number of starting clues for a unique solution proven to be 17. If you have only ever played Sudoku, the good news is that the other five puzzles on this list will feel like new worlds built with the same honest tools.</p>

    <h3>4. Nonogram (Picross)</h3>

    <p>Nonograms, also sold as Picross or Griddlers, are the picture puzzles of the grid family. Along the top and left edges of an empty grid you get number clues, and each clue lists the lengths of consecutive filled runs that must appear in that row or column, in order. Your task is to fill cells until the whole grid resolves into a small picture, usually something charming like a cat or an umbrella. A row of length 10 with the clue "10" fills completely, a clue of "8 1" has eight filled cells, at least one gap, then one more, and working out where the flexibility lies is the heart of the game. Nonograms reward a slightly different muscle than Sudoku: where Sudoku is pure constraint checking, Nonograms mix logic with a running visual hypothesis, you half-see the picture before it is confirmed. They are enormously popular on the Nintendo Switch and phones under the Picross name, and a well-made one delivers a small emotional reward at the end that number puzzles cannot quite match: the finished image.</p>

    <h3>5. Kakuro</h3>

    <p>Kakuro is what happened when someone looked at a crossword and a math puzzle and decided they belonged together. The grid looks like a crossword but the entries are digits 1 through 9, and the clues are sums: a clue of 16 across means the digits in that run add to 16, with no digit repeated within a run. That single twist creates beautiful constraints, because only certain digit combinations can make certain sums. A three-cell run summing to 7 must contain 1, 2, and 4 in some order, no exceptions, and experienced solvers memorize these combination tables the way musicians memorize scales. Kakuro is older than Sudoku in its Japanese home, where it has run in newspapers since the 1950s under the name Kasho Kuro, and it remains the go-to puzzle for people who want their logic with actual arithmetic in it. Fair warning: it is less forgiving than Sudoku at the start, the first few solves feel like wading through syrup, but the moment the combination tables click, it becomes one of the most satisfying grid puzzles there is.</p>

    <h3>6. Slitherlink</h3>

    <p>Slitherlink is the odd one out on this list, and proudly so. Instead of filling cells, you draw a single continuous loop along the dotted lines between them. Numbered cells tell you how many of their four edges the loop uses, a 3 cell has exactly three of its borders on the loop, a 0 cell has none, and the finished loop must close on itself and never cross or touch. The loop wanders, doubles back, and teases you with long stretches of ambiguity before a single edge placement suddenly forces a dozen others. Slitherlink has a reputation as the most spatially intuitive of the Nikoli puzzles: solvers often describe feeling the loop's shape before they can prove it, then working backward to justify what their eyes already knew. It is also the puzzle most likely to give you a genuine "wait, that is it?" moment, when a loop you thought was settled turns out to have been impossible for the last ten minutes. Start with small grids, treat every 0 as a gift, and never trust a loop that has not yet closed.</p>

